# Caddy reverse proxy for self-hosting the all-in-one CourtOps POC image on your
# own VM (DigitalOcean droplet, EC2, etc.). Caddy listens on 443, auto-obtains
# and auto-renews a Let's Encrypt cert, and proxies to the app container, which
# keeps serving plain HTTP on :8000.
#
# Prereqs on the VM:
#   - a domain's A-record pointing at the VM's public IP
#   - ports 80 (ACME challenge) and 443 open
#
# Run the app with a NAMED VOLUME so the embedded DB persists across restarts.
# A fresh volume is empty, so the entrypoint seeds the demo into it on first boot:
#   docker volume create courtops_data
#   docker run -d --name courtops --restart unless-stopped \
#       -v courtops_data:/opt/courtops/pgdata \
#       -e ADMIN_PASSWORD='choose-a-strong-one' \
#       -p 127.0.0.1:8000:8000 ghcr.io/vkozyrev0/tennis-app:latest
#
# The port is published on loopback only: Caddy is the public listener.
# ENV=prod is intentionally absent. The bundled Postgres speaks plaintext on
# loopback with the POC role, and the app's boot guard refuses that combination
# under ENV=prod. A real deployment splits the database out; see docs/deploy.md.
#
# Then run Caddy (host install, or the official caddy image) with this file.
# Replace courtops.example.com with your domain — that's all Caddy needs to get
# a valid cert.

courtops.example.com {
	# Bound uploads at the edge as well as in the app.
	request_body {
		max_size 25MB
	}
	# D26: the all-in-one image cannot run ENV=prod (the boot guard refuses the
	# bundled loopback Postgres with default creds and a non-TLS DSN), so the app
	# does not emit HSTS here. Set it at the edge instead. A split-DB deployment
	# with ENV=prod gets HSTS from the app as well.
	header Strict-Transport-Security "max-age=31536000; includeSubDomains"
	# App already sets CSP / nosniff / X-Frame-Options / Referrer-Policy (D17).
	reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:8000
}
